chunk_findings.detail:
The new helper `_text_refusal` calls `tomllib.loads(text)`:

```python
def _text_refusal(text: str) -> str:
    try:
        data = tomllib.loads(text)
    except tomllib.TOMLDecodeError as exc:
        ...
```

but it has no `import tomllib` in its body. The two functions this diff deletes, `_write_config` and `_append_config`, each carried a function-local `import tomllib` as the very first statement of the body, and this diff removes both while adding no module-level import. On an interpreter where `tomllib` is not bound at module scope, the first line of `_text_refusal` raises `NameError: name 'tomllib' is not defined` before the `TOMLDecodeError` / `appended_key_problem` / `_human_cleared` guards ever run, i.e. every `config --append-toml` / append-block write now crashes instead of doing the intended validate-then-write. Concretely, appending any block (`_text_refusal("x = 1\n")`) reaches `tomllib.loads` and fails with `NameError`, not with `""` or a refusal message. The fix is to restore the local `import tomllib` (or add a module-level one) in the extracted helper.

The guard only walks module-level statements (`ast.parse(...).body`), so any exemption table declared anywhere below the top level escapes it. Concrete case: add to any `tests/test_*.py`

```python
def _help():
    PROSE_TOOLS = {"ddflow_list": "still describes a tool in prose"}
```

or

```python
if TYPE_CHECKING:
    ALIASES = {"a": "b"}
```

`ast.parse(path.read_text()).body` yields only the `FunctionDef`/`If` node, never the inner `Assign`/`AnnAssign`, so `kept` stays empty and the assertion passes even though the file defines exactly the table the docstring promises can no longer exist. It parses the source to enforce "no test keeps its own exemption table", so it should walk nested scopes (e.g. `ast.walk`) or at least reject any `Name` target in the whole tree, not just `body`.
